1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,259
This video shows how to translate a regular expression into an N F A. We use four templates, one for each regex construct.

2
00:00:09,709 --> 00:00:21,591
First we learn the templates. Then we build the N F A for a or a b star, step by step, as the slides do. At the end we solve three questions from the question bank.

3
00:00:22,041 --> 00:00:30,340
We want a program that recognizes the strings of a regular expression. The plan is a small compiler with three steps.

4
00:00:30,790 --> 00:00:41,201
Step 1 translates the regex into an N F A. It uses one template for each construct of the regex. This is the topic of this video.

5
00:00:41,651 --> 00:00:50,781
Step 2 converts the N F A into a D F A, with the subset construction. Step 3 makes the D F A as small as possible.

6
00:00:51,231 --> 00:01:01,706
Each layer is easy to prove correct. Together they are a reference implementation. It is not the fastest method, but it is fast enough most of the time.

7
00:01:02,156 --> 00:01:22,081
The method is syntax-directed translation. The grammar of the input language guides the translation. Almost every translation in this course is of this kind. Step 0: define a translation for each grammar production. This translation does not depend on a particular input.

8
00:01:22,531 --> 00:01:30,041
Step 1: parse the input. Here the input is the regex a or a b star. The result is a parse tree.

9
00:01:30,491 --> 00:01:44,293
Step 2: apply the translations to the parse tree. Start at the bottom and work up. Each node gets a machine, built from the machines of its children. The numbers show how many states each machine has.

10
00:01:44,743 --> 00:01:51,207
Step 3: the result is the translation of the root. Here it is an N F A with 10 states.

11
00:01:51,657 --> 00:02:07,401
Regular expressions have four constructs, plus the empty string. A constant is one thing. Concatenation is one thing after another. Alternation is one thing or another. Repetition is one thing, zero or more times.

12
00:02:07,851 --> 00:02:12,950
The question mark and the plus are syntactic sugar. They are not new constructs.

13
00:02:13,400 --> 00:02:27,011
x question mark is x or epsilon. x plus is x x star. Rewrite them before you build the machine. In x x star, x appears two times. Each copy gets its own states.

14
00:02:27,461 --> 00:02:37,125
We need one translation per construct. The translations produce N F As, because we use epsilon edges as glue between the parts.

15
00:02:37,575 --> 00:02:49,073
Template 1 is for a constant. It is just an edge between two states, labelled with the constant. State 1 is the entry. State 2 is the exit, and it accepts.

16
00:02:49,523 --> 00:02:56,798
The empty string uses the same shape. The edge is an epsilon edge. The course notes draw it this way.

17
00:02:57,248 --> 00:03:15,637
This is the invariant. Every machine that the templates build has exactly one entry state and one exit state. The exit is the accepting state. Because of this, we can plug any machine into any template as one unit. We draw such a machine as a box M.

18
00:03:16,087 --> 00:03:26,050
Template 2 is concatenation, x y. Mx is the machine for x, and My is the machine for y. Either one may be more than a constant.

19
00:03:26,500 --> 00:03:42,543
Join the two machines end to end. An epsilon edge goes from the exit of Mx to the entry of My. The entry of the new machine is the entry of Mx. The exit is the exit of My. State 2 no longer accepts.

20
00:03:42,993 --> 00:03:55,259
Here it is for two constants, x then y. State 2 goes to state 3 on epsilon. This video uses the epsilon edge, as the slides do in the worked example.

21
00:03:55,709 --> 00:04:08,189
You can also merge the two states. The exit of Mx becomes the entry of My. The slides say this is the same thing. Both machines accept exactly the string x y.

22
00:04:08,639 --> 00:04:14,378
Template 3 is alternation, x or y. Put the two machines side by side.

23
00:04:14,828 --> 00:04:21,655
Add two new states. New 1 is the entry of the whole machine. New 2 is the exit, and it accepts.

24
00:04:22,105 --> 00:04:28,910
From new 1, two epsilon edges go to the entries of Mx and My. The oracle picks the branch.

25
00:04:29,360 --> 00:04:41,584
From the exits of Mx and My, two epsilon edges go to new 2. So the template adds 2 new states and 4 new epsilon edges. The old exits no longer accept.

26
00:04:42,034 --> 00:04:52,530
Here it is for two constants. State 5 is new 1, and state 6 is new 2. A string is accepted if it goes through the x branch or the y branch.

27
00:04:52,980 --> 00:04:58,441
Template 4 is repetition, x star. It puts a loop around the machine M.

28
00:04:58,891 --> 00:05:10,454
Add two new states, new 1 and new 2. An epsilon edge goes from new 1 into M, and one from the exit of M to new 2. This path runs M one time.

29
00:05:10,904 --> 00:05:18,371
A third epsilon edge goes from new 1 directly to new 2. It skips M. This gives zero copies.

30
00:05:18,821 --> 00:05:31,770
A fourth epsilon edge goes from the exit of M back to its entry. It runs M again. So again 2 new states and 4 new epsilon edges: skip it, run it, run it again.

31
00:05:32,220 --> 00:05:38,833
Here it is for the constant x. The empty string takes the skip edge, from 3 directly to 4.

32
00:05:39,283 --> 00:05:48,051
The string x x goes 3, 1, 2, then back to 1, then 2 again, then 4. Each trip around the loop reads one more x.

33
00:05:48,501 --> 00:05:53,792
Now the exercise from the slides. Build the N F A for a or a b star.

34
00:05:54,242 --> 00:06:06,637
First, parse the regex. The star binds tightest. Then concatenation, then alternation. So the tree is: alternation of a, and the concatenation of a with b star.

35
00:06:07,087 --> 00:06:27,716
Build from the bottom up. The three constants come first, from left to right. They get states 1 to 6. Then each operator node gets its template, from the bottom up. The repetition adds 7 and 8. The concatenation adds an epsilon edge from 4 to 7. The alternation adds 9 and 10.

36
00:06:28,166 --> 00:06:33,798
Do not think about whether the result is right. Only match each piece against its template.

37
00:06:34,248 --> 00:06:38,216
Start with the atoms. The first a gets states 1 and 2.

38
00:06:38,666 --> 00:06:45,877
The second a gets states 3 and 4. The b gets states 5 and 6. These are three constant machines.

39
00:06:46,327 --> 00:06:52,513
Each one has one entry and one exit. So each one can go into a template as a unit.

40
00:06:52,963 --> 00:07:01,155
Next, the lowest operator: the repetition b star. It uses template 4 around the b machine, states 5 and 6.

41
00:07:01,605 --> 00:07:08,560
Add the new states 7 and 8. Epsilon edges go from 7 into 5, and from 6 out to 8.

42
00:07:09,010 --> 00:07:19,549
Add the skip edge from 7 to 8, below, and the loop back edge from 6 to 5, above. Now the machine for b star has entry 7 and exit 8.

43
00:07:19,999 --> 00:07:26,356
Next, the concatenation a b star. It joins the second a machine and the b star machine.

44
00:07:26,806 --> 00:07:39,393
Template 2: an epsilon edge glues the exit of the a machine, state 4, to the entry of b star, state 7. The machine for a b star has entry 3 and exit 8.

45
00:07:39,843 --> 00:07:43,491
Last, the root: the alternation of a and a b star.

46
00:07:43,941 --> 00:07:52,815
Template 3 adds a new entry, state 9. Two epsilon edges go to the entries of the two branches, states 1 and 3.

47
00:07:53,265 --> 00:08:07,921
It also adds a new accepting state, 10. Epsilon edges go to it from both exits, 2 and 8. This is the N F A for a or a b star. Ten states, start state 9, accepting state 10.

48
00:08:08,371 --> 00:08:22,707
The machine is ugly to execute. It has epsilon moves and choices everywhere. But it is easy to check. Each piece matches a template, so it is correct by construction. A wrong arrow is easy to see.

49
00:08:23,157 --> 00:08:35,403
Test it anyway. The string a b b has a path to 10: 9, 3, 4, 7, 5, 6, back to 5, 6, 8, 10. So the N F A accepts a b b.

50
00:08:35,853 --> 00:08:46,306
The string b a has no path. From 9, the epsilon edges reach only 1 and 3, and neither has a b edge. So the N F A rejects b a.

51
00:08:46,756 --> 00:08:54,991
To run this machine, a program must follow every choice. The next video removes the choices with the subset construction.

52
00:08:55,441 --> 00:09:10,438
One more fact from the slides. a or a b star is the same language as a b star, because a is in a b star. It is hard to argue this directly. The pipeline finds it: the minimal D F A has 2 states.

53
00:09:10,888 --> 00:09:27,037
The templates give a quick check. Each constant, and each epsilon, adds 2 states and 1 edge. Concatenation adds 1 epsilon edge and no states. Alternation and repetition each add 2 states and 4 epsilon edges.

54
00:09:27,487 --> 00:09:34,549
For a or a b star: 3 constants, 1 concatenation, 1 alternation and 1 repetition.

55
00:09:34,999 --> 00:09:47,756
So there are 2 times 5, which is 10 states, and 3 plus 1 plus 4 plus 4, which is 12 edges. That matches the drawing. Use this count to check your drawing on the exam.

56
00:09:48,206 --> 00:10:09,497
Now the question bank. Question 4.3.1. Draw an N F A for R question mark, which matches zero or one copy of R. Then draw one for R plus, which matches one or more copies of R. The bank gives no figure, so this is our solution. Hint: use the rewrite rules from the slides.

57
00:10:09,947 --> 00:10:11,589
Pause the video and try it.

58
00:10:12,039 --> 00:10:20,743
Part a. Rewrite R question mark as R or epsilon. The box is the machine for R, with entry 1 and exit 2.

59
00:10:21,193 --> 00:10:27,572
Epsilon is a constant. Its machine is two states with an epsilon edge, 3 to 4.

60
00:10:28,022 --> 00:10:45,302
Now apply template 3, alternation. New state 5 is the entry, with epsilon edges to 1 and 3. New state 6 is the exit, with epsilon edges from 2 and 4. The lower path reads nothing, so it gives zero copies of R.

61
00:10:45,752 --> 00:11:00,877
Check with R equal to a. The machine has 6 states and 6 edges, as the count says. It accepts the empty string and a, and nothing else. With R equal to a b, it accepts the empty string and a b only.

62
00:11:01,327 --> 00:11:04,655
Part b. Rewrite R plus as R R star.

63
00:11:05,105 --> 00:11:09,820
The first R is a plain copy of the machine for R, states 1 and 2.

64
00:11:10,270 --> 00:11:21,427
The second R is a new copy, states 3 and 4. Put template 4 around it: new states 5 and 6, the skip edge above, and the back edge from 4 to 3.

65
00:11:21,877 --> 00:11:41,141
Template 2 joins them: an epsilon edge from 2 to 5. A string must go through the first copy, so it has at least one copy of R. Check with R equal to a: 6 states and 7 edges. It accepts a, a a, a a a, and so on, but not the empty string.

66
00:11:41,591 --> 00:11:52,706
Question 4.3.2. Draw an N F A for a or b, star, then a, then a or b, cubed. The bank gives no figure, so this is our solution.

67
00:11:53,156 --> 00:11:54,799
Pause the video and try it.

68
00:11:55,249 --> 00:12:07,771
The power 3 is not a new construct. It means three copies: a or b, a or b, a or b. So the regex is a chain of five parts, joined by concatenation.

69
00:12:08,221 --> 00:12:19,101
Count first. There are 9 constants, 4 alternations and 1 repetition. So the template N F A has 2 times 14, which is 28 states.

70
00:12:19,551 --> 00:12:29,706
Build it part by part. First the inner a or b. The constants a and b get states 1 to 4. The alternation adds 19 and 20.

71
00:12:30,156 --> 00:12:40,695
Then the repetition around it adds 21 and 22, with the skip edge above and the back edge from 20 to 19. State 21 is the start.

72
00:12:41,145 --> 00:12:48,035
Then the constant a, states 5 and 6. An epsilon edge from 22 to 5 glues it on.

73
00:12:48,485 --> 00:12:59,600
Then the first copy of a or b, states 7 to 10, with the new states 23 and 24. An epsilon edge from 6 to 23 glues it on.

74
00:13:00,050 --> 00:13:16,562
The second and third copies work the same way. State 28 is the only accepting state. That is 28 states, as the count said. A python check ran this machine on every string up to length 9. It agrees with the regex.

75
00:13:17,012 --> 00:13:32,137
The question asks for any N F A. The templates always give a correct one. A smaller N F A is also correct, if you check it. Here state 0 loops on a and b. Then an a, then exactly three more letters.

76
00:13:32,587 --> 00:13:44,875
Run the string b a a b a. State 0 reads b and stays. On the first a, the path goes to 1. Then a, b, a take it to 2, 3 and 4. State 4 accepts.

77
00:13:45,325 --> 00:13:55,672
From state 0, the letter a has two edges, to 0 and to 1. This is the nondeterminism. The oracle guesses the a that is fourth from the end.

78
00:13:56,122 --> 00:14:14,469
This machine has 5 states. A python check compared it with the regex and with the 28 state machine, on every string up to length 11. If you draw a small machine by hand, test it like this. The templates need no test, because each piece matches a template.

79
00:14:14,919 --> 00:14:23,943
Question 4.3.3. Construct an N F A for this regex. The bank gives no figure, so this is our solution.

80
00:14:24,393 --> 00:14:26,035
Pause the video and try it.

81
00:14:26,485 --> 00:14:46,133
A large regex is easier if you give names to its parts. Call a or b, b, b star the part X. Call a a star the part Z. Call a or b, a, b the part Y. Then the regex is X star or Z, followed by Y star. This is the top of the parse tree.

82
00:14:46,583 --> 00:14:54,925
Build the part X first. a or b uses the alternation template: constants 1 to 4, and new states 9 and 10.

83
00:14:55,375 --> 00:15:01,860
Then the constant b, states 5 and 6. An epsilon edge from 10 to 5 joins it.

84
00:15:02,310 --> 00:15:13,873
Then b star: the constant b, states 7 and 8, inside template 4 with new states 11 and 12. An epsilon edge from 6 to 11 joins it.

85
00:15:14,323 --> 00:15:22,024
The part X has 12 states. The numbers here are local to this part. In the whole machine, the states get other numbers.

86
00:15:22,474 --> 00:15:31,562
Now put the parts together, from the bottom of the tree up. Template 4 around X gives X star. Two new states, so 14 states.

87
00:15:32,012 --> 00:15:41,484
Z is a a star. It is built like X and has 6 states. The alternation of X star and Z adds a new entry and a new exit.

88
00:15:41,934 --> 00:16:00,601
Y is a or b, a, b, with 10 states. Template 4 around Y gives Y star, with 12 states. Last, an epsilon edge glues the exit of the alternation to the entry of Y star. The exit of Y star is the only accepting state.

89
00:16:01,051 --> 00:16:22,427
The total is 14 plus 6 plus 2 plus 12, which is 34 states. The count rule gives the same: 2 times 10 constants, 3 alternations and 4 repetitions. A python build of the templates also gives 34 states, and it matches the regex on every string up to length 8.

90
00:16:22,877 --> 00:16:34,141
Thirty four states is a lot to draw in an exam. Here is a smaller N F A, our solution, made by hand. State 1 is the start. State 6 accepts.

91
00:16:34,591 --> 00:16:47,455
The cycle 2, 3, 4 is X star. From 2, a or b goes to 3, then b goes to 4, and 4 loops on b. The epsilon edge from 4 back to 2 starts the next X.

92
00:16:47,905 --> 00:16:58,358
The a edge from 1 to 5, and the a loop on 5, make a a star. No edge goes into state 1. So after an X, the machine cannot continue with Z.

93
00:16:58,808 --> 00:17:09,048
The cycle 6, 7, 8 is Y star: a or b, then a, then b, back to 6. State 6 accepts, so zero copies of Y are allowed.

94
00:17:09,498 --> 00:17:21,039
This machine has 8 states. A python check compared it with the regex and with the 34 state template machine, on every string up to length 11. They agree.

95
00:17:21,489 --> 00:17:32,945
To summarize. Regex to N F A is a syntax-directed translation. Parse the regex, then apply one template per node of the tree, from the bottom up.

96
00:17:33,395 --> 00:17:40,329
Rewrite the sugar first. x question mark is x or epsilon. x plus is x x star.

97
00:17:40,779 --> 00:17:48,779
A constant is two states with one edge. Every machine has one entry and one exit, so the machines plug together.

98
00:17:49,229 --> 00:17:59,938
Concatenation adds one epsilon edge, or merges two states. Alternation and repetition each add 2 new states and 4 new epsilon edges.

99
00:18:00,388 --> 00:18:16,196
Check your work. Count 2 states for each constant, each alternation and each repetition. If you draw a smaller machine by hand, test it against the regex. Next video: N F A to D F A, the subset construction.
